Why do we set $x$ to $1$ ?
Answer 


Let us manipulate the equation $f(x+f(x))=4f(x)$:
\begin{align*}
f(x+f(x))&=4f(x) 
\\(x+f(x)) + f(x+f(x)) &=4f(x) + f(x+f(x)) \\
(x+f(x)) + f(x+f(x)) & = 4f(x) + 4f(x) 
\\f(x+f(x)) + f(x+f(x)) &= 8f(x) \\
2f(x+f(x)) &= 8f(x)\\
f(x+f(x)) &= 4f(x) \text{, as before.}
\end{align*}
Now it looks like a recursive function. Let us set $f(x) = 4k$:
\begin{align*} 
f(k) = 4\cdot 4k = 4\cdot f(x) \Rightarrow f(k) &= 4f(x) \text{, or } f(f(x)) = 4 f(x)
\end{align*}
So we have the following:
\begin{align*}
f(x+f(x))&=4f(x) \\
f(f(x)) &= 4f(x) \\
f(1) &= f(f(1)) = 4f(1) = 16 
\end{align*}
Now we want $f(21)$. It is useful to note that $x = 8k$ seems to fit the equation. So let us try it:
\begin{align*} 
f(8k) &= (f(k) + f(k)) + (f(k) + f(k)) + (f(k) + f(k)) + (f(k) + f(k)) + 
f(k) + f(k) \\
&= 8 (f(k) + f(k))  \\
&= 8f(2k) \\
f(8) &= 8 f(2) \\
&= 128 \\
f(16) &= 8 f(4) \\
&= 512 \\
f(32) &= 8 f(8) \\
&= 1024 \\
\end{align*}
This does not seem to be a pattern. So let us try $x = 4k$ instead:
\begin{align*} 
f(4k) &= f(f(2k)) + f(f(2k)) + f(f(2k)) + f(f(2k)) + f(f(2k))
\\&= 4f(f(2k))  \\
f(4) &= 4 f(f(2))  \\
f(8) &= 4 f(4) 
\end{align*}
This is much closer to the pattern that we saw earlier, i.e. $f(8) = 8 f(2)$, so let us try $k = 2$:
\begin{align*} 
f(4\cdot2) &= 4f(2\cdot 2) = 16f(2)  \\
96 &= 16f(2) \Rightarrow f(2) = 6
\end{align*}
So we know $f(8)$ and the pattern that happened to fit when $x = 4k$:
\begin{align*} 
f(8) &=4 f(4)  \\
&= 16f(2)  \\
&= 96 
\end{align*}
Now we try $x = 16$:
\begin{align*}
f(16) &= 4f(8) \\
&= \boxed{384} 
\end{align*}

